2.3.4. Mass flow rate distribution
The mass flow rate distribution have great influence on the mixing, which in turn influence the combustion. Similar with the distribution of SMD and mean axial velocity, the mass flow rate distribution (or the mass/volume flux) shows ‘single peak’ shape, as shown in Fig. 17. It is because that the primary breakup and secondary atomization produces a large amount of droplets around the stream line of the liquid film. And these droplets have larger SMD and mean axial velocity than the center and the periphery of the spray. Santolaya et al. [103,127,153] found that the liquid flow rate is concentrated in a small region where the droplets presented a high dispersion of sizes. And the high collision rates of droplets in the dense spray could redistribute the liquid mass flow rate. Koh et al. [156] measured the mass flow rate from laser induced fluorescence images, and found it agrees well with the mass flow rate measured by PDPA.
2.4. Effects of the rocket engine environment
In the liquid rocket engine, the chamber pressure and the combustion gas temperature are really high, which indicates that the atomization occurs with the back pressure and high temperature. If the pressure exceeds the critical pressure and the temperature exceeds the critical temperature, the propellant is supercritical. The atomization of propellant at supercritical conditions is quite different from that at normal condition. In this section, the effects of high back pressure and temperature on the inner flow characteristics of the pressure swirl injector and the atomization of the conical liquid film are reviewed.
2.4.1. Effects of back pressure
First, the effects of back pressure on the internal flow characteristics of pressure swirl injector. Kenny et al. [49,50] found that the increase of back pressure increases the film thickness if the mass flow rate keeps constant. And the corresponding discharge coefficient also increases because the increase of film thickness decreases the axial velocity of the liquid film, thus the friction loss decreases sequentially. He also found that the back pressure significantly influence the internal swirling flow that several “necks” arise on the air core, and the dimension and location of these necks vary with the back pressure and tends to a constant. Chen et al. [157] simulated the internal flow of pressure swirl injector with back pressure, and found that the increase of film thickness with the back pressure is caused by the intensified gas liquid shear stress that changes the velocity profile. He also captured the stationary surface waves that looks like the necks in experiment, and the location of the stationary surface waves agree well with that of the necks.
Second, the effects of back pressure on the breakup of the conical liquid film. The back pressure increases the gas density and enhance the gas liquid interaction, which significantly promote the primary breakup of the liquid film, as shown in Fig. 24. The experimental results of Kenny et al. [49,50] show that the spray cone angle decreases with the increase of the back pressure. The simulation results of Chen et al. [157] also show that the back pressure converges the conical liquid film and decreases the spray cone angle. What's more, the research of Moon et al. [102], Fu and Yang [125] and Eberhart et al. [126] all indicates that the spray cone angle decreases with the increase of the back pressure. Kim et al. [122] further figured out that the back pressure has little influence on the spray cone angle before the breakup of the conical liquid film and significantly decreases the spray cone angle after that. Although the back pressure has little influence on the cone angle of the liquid film, it greatly affects the primary breakup for the enhancement of the gas liquid interaction. Moon et al. [102] found that the breakup of the conical liquid film is dominated by long wave mode at atmospheric pressure, and is dominated by short wave mode with back pressure, because the back pressure increases the Webber number and enhances the gas liquid interaction. Kim et al. [122] found that the amplitude of the disturbance increases and the breakup length decreases with the increase of the liquid Webber number and back pressure, because the aerodynamic force increases. All the research of Moon et al. [102], Fu and Yang [125] and Eberhart et al. [126] show that the breakup length decreases with the increase of back pressure, indicating that the back pressure promotes the primary breakup.
