Third, the effects of back pressure on the atomization characteristics. Eberhart et al. [126] found that the mean axial velocity and droplet size decrease with the increase of back pressure in the primary breakup region. Elbadawy et al. [158] found that it is the increased turbulence level of the ambient gas decreases the SMD, increases the radial penetration of the vapor and increases the droplets number.
2.4.2. Effects of temperature
In liquid rocket engines, the temperature of the propellants in the manifolds are smaller than the critical point. And the propellants is heated by the hot gas in the combustion chamber during injection, and may exceeds the critical temperature. When the temperature and pressure in the combustion chamber exceeds the critical point of the propellants, the pressure swirl injector works at supercritical condition. It means that the atomization may occurs from subcritical condition to supercritical condition. Thus the temperature have significant influence on the atomization of pressure swirl injector.
The supercritical fluid do not exist distinct liquid and gas phases, and do not have gas liquid interface. Thus there is no surface tension in a supercritical fluid. By changing the pressure and temperature of the fluid, the properties can be “tuned” to be more liquid-like or more gas-like. And close to the critical point, small changes in pressure or temperature result in large changes in density. It means that the injection of supercritical propellants is quite different from that of liquid propellants. The conical liquid film disappears and the injection is more like a gas injection. The typical subcritical spray and supercritical spray of a pressure swirl injector are shown in Fig. 25. Cho et al. [159] studied the surface instability of cryogen swirl flow at subcritical to supercritical conditions. And found that the spray cone angle, the wavelength and the propagation velocity are drastically changed by the phase change. He figured out that at subcritical condition the instability of the cryogenic swirl flow is dominated by the precessing vortex core in the central recirculation zone. And the increase of back pressure decreases the flow velocity, which in turn decreases the frequency of the instability. However, at subcritical to supercritical condition, the frequency do not change drastically because the instability of the sub-to-supercritical flow is determined by the flow in a liquid state.
