2.5. Response of injector and atomization on the pressure oscillation
When the combustion instability occurs, either the pressure in the supply system or the pressure in the combustion chamber oscillates. These pressure oscillations could induce the spray oscillation and then couple with the combustion. In order to eliminate the combustion instability, the injector dynamics and the response characteristics of the primary breakup and atomization on the pressure oscillation are needed.
2.5.1. Injector dynamics
The pressure oscillation in the supply system could transmit in the injector, causing the film thickness and velocity at the injector exit fluctuate. And the film thickness and velocity oscillation will further influence the primary breakup of the conical liquid film. To understand the effects of the pressure oscillation on the primary breakup, the injector dynamics that describe the response characteristics of the parameters at the injector exit to the pressure oscillation at the injector inlet are needed.
In the theoretical research, Bazarov [160] conducted the pioneer works. He developed a linear analytic model based on the long-wave disturbances traveling on the free surface of the injector core, and analyzed the dynamics of a classical pressure swirl injector. Fu [66] found that the linear model is suitable for the low frequency combustion instability, because the disturbance propagation velocity is obtained by regarding the disturbance waves as long waves. This is true only when the disturbance wavelength is much larger than the injector length. When high frequency combustion instability occurs, the disturbance waves cannot be regarded as long waves, because the disturbance wavelength is in the same level with the injector size. And the disturbance propagation velocity is related with the frequency. Fu [66] corrected the disturbance propagation velocity by including the dispersion equation of the disturbance waves. Based on the corrected model, he investigated the dynamics of an open end swirl injector, and obtained the transfer function of the injector.
In the numerical research, the research group of Heister [131,[161], [162], [163], [164], [165], [166], [167]] in Purdue university have conducted a lot of simulation on the nonlinear dynamic response of the pressure swirl injector with the boundary element method (BEM). Ismailov et al. [161,162,165,166,168] dissected the entire unsteady flow in swirl injectors into the disturbance wave refraction, wave reflection, and vortex chamber resonance, and conducted the linear small perturbation analysis based on these assumptions. He also conducted the dynamic simulation with the nonlinear boundary element method and found that the dynamic responses peak at the frequency that are coincident with the resonant frequency. Richardson et al. [79,167] compared the simulation results of boundary element method with that of Bazarov's linear model, and found that they agree well when the frequency is smaller than 5000 Hz, after that the deviation occurs.
In the experimental research, two special techniques are required. One is the pulsator that induce the pressure fluctuation, the other is the quantification of the transient mass flow rate at the injector exit. At present hydromechanical pulsator is widely used, Wilson et al. [169,170] and Bazarov et al. [171] have reviewed and investigated the characteristics of the hydromechanical pulsators, and compared the performance. The transient mass flow rate at the injector exit cannot be measured directly, and the most used method is to measure the transient film thickness at the injector exit and then calculate the transient mass flow rate with the transient film thickness and the average film velocity. Fu et al. [39,41] found that the frequency of the film thickness fluctuation coincides with that of the pressure oscillation. And the amplitude of the film thickness fluctuation decreases with the increase of the pressure oscillation frequency. He also figured out that the phase contrast between the mass flow rate fluctuation and the pressure oscillation increases linearly with the increase of the pressure oscillation frequency. What's more, the phase contrast increases with the increase of the geometry characteristics constant A, and decreases with the increase of the pressure drop. Ahn et al. [172,173] found that the water hammer effects exists when the pressure oscillates, and it affects the resonant peak to deviate the theoretical value. And if the water hammer effects is accounted, the measured resonant peak agrees well with the theoretical value. Jin and Yoon [174] found that the gain of the normalized transfer function increases with the oscillation frequency while the phase contrast of that decreases. However, the gain decreases with the oscillation frequency at around 100 130 Hz because the acoustic characteristics of the fuel feed line may control the flow. Khil et al. [42] measured the pressure, film thickness, axial velocity and mass flow rate at the injector exit, and found that the phase diagrams of the pressure, axial velocity and mass flow rate started from the fourth quadrant and moved in a clockwise direction to quadrants 3, 2, 1. However, the phase diagram of the film thickness started in the first quadrant. He also figured out that the average injection pressure has little influence on the phase diagram, but it decreases the amplitude of the fluctuation because it decreases the relative magnitude of the fluctuated amplitude and the average injection pressure. What's interesting in his research is that the fluctuation amplitude decreases with the increase of oscillation frequency between 0 and 75 Hz, while the opposite trend occurs when the oscillation frequency exceeds 75 Hz. He think that this phenomenon is caused by the Klystron effect.
The injector dynamics can be significantly influenced by the injector geometry. Chung et al. [175] investigated the effects of the diameter and length of the swirl chamber, and found that the mass flow rate oscillation is weakened by the decrease of the swirl chamber diameter and the increase of the swirl chamber length. It indicates that the pressure swirl injector with a slender swirl chamber is dynamically more stable and is better for combustion. The row number of the tangential inlets is another important geometrical parameter. Fu [66] derived the transfer function of a pressure swirl injector with double rows of tangential ports, and found that the oscillation is damped when the distance between the rows equal to half a disturbance wavelength, because the surface waves induced by these two rows of inlet are in opposite phase. Richardson et al. [79,167] also found that the open-end injector with double rows of inlets have better stability characteristics than the injector with a single row of inlets. Zakharov et al. [176] also investigated the mechanism of wave canceling in pressure swirl injector with multi rows of tangential inlets.
As for the open-end swirl injector, Fu et al. [39] found that the amplitude-frequency variation and the phase-frequency variation is similar with that of a classical pressure swirl injector. And the larger the pressure drop, the larger the amplitude and the smaller the phase contrast. He also figured out that the swirl injector that is more slender and has larger geometry characteristic constant A can damp more oscillation energy. Chung et al. [45] investigated the effects of the diameter and length of the swirl chamber on the dynamic characteristics of an open-end swirl injector. He found that the amplitude of the film thickness fluctuation increases with the increase of the pressure oscillation amplitude. And the slope decreases with the increase of the swirl chamber diameter, the swirl chamber length and the tangential inlet number, and decreases with the decrease of the manifold diameter. Furthermore, the amplitude of the liquid film thickness peak at a pulsation frequency of 200 Hz, and the amplitude of this peak decreases with the increase of the swirl chamber diameter and length. Correspondingly, the range of the spray cone angle oscillation peak at the pulsation frequency 200 Hz.
2.5.2. Response of film breakup and atomization on the pressure oscillation
When low frequency combustion instability occurs, the pressure in the supply system oscillates and affects the primary breakup of the conical liquid film, as shown in Fig. 26. Kim and Lai [177] found that the pressure oscillation can promote the growth of the surface waves when the pressure drop is low. The spray becomes more uniform, the droplet number density and volume flux increased about 30%, because the primary breakup of the conical liquid film is accelerated by the promoted surface waves.
